1. Visual Monitoring Setup Meeting
Main topics covered

A. Regression visual monitoring cleanup

Regression currently has some monitors that are not working.
Action item: review and clean up the regression environment so monitoring is reliable.
The goal is to detect problems earlier instead of discovering them only in production.
B. How Dynatrace HTTP monitors appear on the dashboard

The important concept is the AMD portal-* tag.

Each HTTP synthetic monitor needs the appropriate AMD portal-* tag to determine which dashboard pane/tile it appears in.
Example:
AMD portal-*
AMD portal-EVV LTC
The availability tags such as AMDR, availability level 1, etc. control other monitoring characteristics.

Key takeaway:
If an existing monitor is working but appears in the wrong dashboard pane, you generally change its tags rather than create another monitor.

C. Creating a new HTTP synthetic monitor

The standard process discussed was:

Go to Synthetic monitoring.
Create an HTTP monitor.
Give it a name / URL.
Add the required tags.
Configure the HTTP request.
Use the standard settings:
Accept any SSL certificate.
Timeout around 60 seconds.
Treat the required success status appropriately.
Select the appropriate location.
Use approximately 2-minute execution frequency for dashboard monitoring.
Create the monitor.
Add the appropriate AMD portal-* tag.
Allow several minutes / an execution cycle for it to appear on the dashboard.
D. Moving an existing monitor

If a monitor needs to move from one dashboard tile to another:

Remove the existing AMD portal-* tag and add the appropriate new one.

You don't need to create a new monitor.

E. Adding new servers to Dynatrace

The meeting also introduced a server onboarding tracker.

The tracker should contain:

New servers
Servers being retired
Firewall status
Installation status
Notes
Production/non-production status

The tracker should be moved to the shared SharePoint/Dynatrace location so everyone uses the same source.

2. Production Server Configuration Meeting

This file goes deeper into the new server → Dynatrace production onboarding process.

Server onboarding process

The discussion establishes roughly this flow:

Step 1 — Identify and obtain approval

For each server, gather information such as:

Server name
Production vs. non-production
CPU
Memory
OS
Whether Full Stack monitoring is required
Whether the new server replaces an existing server

When a new server replaces an old one, it's better to mention both the replacement and retirement in the same approval request. This helps with licensing justification.

Step 2 — Firewall request

After approval:

Send the approved server list to the appropriate team.
Obtain the required Dynatrace ActiveGate information.
Determine the firewall rule/port requirements.
The discussion specifically references port 9999.
Submit and track the firewall request.

Firewall completion should be recorded in the tracker.

Step 3 — Obtain OneAgent installation information

After the firewall is implemented:

Request the appropriate Dynatrace OneAgent download/install commands.
Commands differ for Windows vs. Linux.
The installation command contains environment/server-specific information.
Linux requires additional involvement/access, including the discussion's reference to PAD.
Step 4 — Security/software process

The process has changed from the older approach.

Previously, the team could download and install the agent directly.

Now the process involves:

Security/software download process
Providing the appropriate software location
Security review/process
Obtaining the approved installation package/commands

The meeting specifically notes that the software is associated with an AMD software location.

Step 5 — Install OneAgent

Once prerequisites are complete:

Install the Dynatrace OneAgent.
For Linux, coordinate with the required team/person.
For production, required change/TFS/PPM approval must be completed before installation.
Step 6 — Enable monitoring

A very important licensing point was discussed:

Adding a server to Dynatrace does not necessarily consume monitoring/data usage if monitoring is disabled.

Therefore, the recommended approach for a replacement server is:

Add the new server ahead of time.
Keep monitoring disabled.
When the application actually switches to the new server:
Disable the old server.
Enable the new server.

This avoids prematurely consuming monitoring data.

Step 7 — Validate

After installation/enabling:

Wait a few minutes.
Check the Dynatrace dashboard.
Confirm the host/OneAgent is reporting.
Validate that monitoring is functioning correctly.

The meeting specifically discusses checking the dashboard after roughly 2–5 minutes.

3. RUM / Application Monitoring

The second major section is about Real User Monitoring (RUM).

Application detection

RUM applications are associated through application detection rules, commonly using URL/host matching.

For example:

secure.tmhp.com → secure application
operations.tmhp.org → operations application
A specific PA URL can be configured so that RUM applies specifically to that application.

The important distinction is:

Entire-site RUM

Turning RUM on at the site/domain level can enable it for all applications under that site.

Application-level RUM

Using an application detection rule allows RUM to be configured for a specific application/URL.

The meeting emphasizes being careful with secure sites because of their high traffic volume.

4. Full Stack vs Infrastructure Monitoring

The meeting also covers changing the host monitoring mode.

From the host:

Settings → Host monitoring → Monitoring mode

You can select between:

Infrastructure monitoring
Full Stack monitoring

Any change should be recorded in the tracker.

